<quiz>

<!-- ============================================================
     PROTOTYPE: Pointon/Sangwin "type 3 — classify"
     The student sorts objects into categories.

     EXEMPLAR TASK: Sort four functions into two groups —
     those that have a stationary point, and those that do not.
         x^2 - 4x  -> HAS a stationary point (minimum at x = 2)
         3x + 2    -> NO stationary point (linear)
         x^2 + 1   -> HAS a stationary point (minimum at x = 0)
         5 - 2x    -> NO stationary point (linear)

     INPUT MECHANISM CHOSEN: one dropdown per object.
       Each function gets a dropdown with the two category labels
       ("Has a stationary point" / "No stationary point").

     WHY NOT DRAG-AND-DROP (Parsons) GROUPING:
       Our STACK Parsons is wired through prooflib.mac
       (proof(), proof_assessment, proof_alternatives), which grades a
       single ORDERED sequence of steps. It has no robust grading path
       for sorting objects into multiple labelled buckets. The Parsons
       UI can show extra columns, but there is no supported
       bucket-classification answer test in our build, so grading would
       rely on hand-decoding parsons_decode() output — fragile and
       untested. Per our dropdown-discipline rule, dropdowns ARE the
       correct input for fixed 2-4 option classification. A robust
       dropdown classify that validates beats a flaky drag-and-drop.

     PRT DESIGN:
       Node 0  — full marks only if ALL FOUR are classified correctly.
       Node 1  — diagnostic: fires if a LINEAR function was called
                 "Has a stationary point" (the core misconception),
                 with feedback naming the underlying idea.
                 Otherwise a generic hint.

     This question is intentionally FIXED (specific named functions),
     like a proof-structure prototype. The four functions are chosen
     to be UNAMBIGUOUS (quadratics with a clear minimum vs linear
     functions), avoiding cubics/inflections. No figure is shown on
     purpose: a graph would give the answer away.
     ============================================================ -->
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Classify objects prototype - stationary points</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[
<p class="tb-exercise">Exercise</p>
<p>Sort these functions into two groups: those that <strong>have a stationary point</strong>, and those that do <strong>not</strong>.</p>
<p>A stationary point is a point where the gradient (the derivative) is zero. For each function, choose the correct group.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><div>\(f(x) = x^2 - 4x\) &nbsp; [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div></li>
  <li><div>\(f(x) = 3x + 2\) &nbsp; [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div></li>
  <li><div>\(f(x) = x^2 + 1\) &nbsp; [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</div></li>
  <li><div>\(f(x) = 5 - 2x\) &nbsp; [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]</div></li>
</ol>
    ]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[
<p>A function has a stationary point exactly where its derivative is zero. Differentiate each function and ask whether the derivative can equal zero.</p>
<ul>
  <li><p>\(f(x) = x^2 - 4x\): here \(f'(x) = 2x - 4\), which is zero when \(x = 2\). So this function <strong>has a stationary point</strong> (a minimum at \(x = 2\)).</p></li>
  <li><p>\(f(x) = 3x + 2\): here \(f'(x) = 3\), a constant that is never zero. A linear function has a constant gradient, so it has <strong>no stationary point</strong>.</p></li>
  <li><p>\(f(x) = x^2 + 1\): here \(f'(x) = 2x\), which is zero when \(x = 0\). So this function <strong>has a stationary point</strong> (a minimum at \(x = 0\)).</p></li>
  <li><p>\(f(x) = 5 - 2x\): here \(f'(x) = -2\), a constant that is never zero. So this linear function has <strong>no stationary point</strong>.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The key idea: linear functions have a constant, non-zero gradient, so their derivative is never zero and they never have stationary points. The quadratics turn around at their vertex, where the gradient is momentarily zero.</p>
    ]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026080600</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[
/* Two category labels, same DISPLAY for every function.
   Each option is [value, correct, display]: the value is a unique
   short code (so the four dropdowns never share a value), the display
   is the human-readable category label, the boolean marks the correct
   category for that function. Unique values avoid any cross-dropdown
   value-resolution ambiguity in the grader. */
opt1: [["sp1", true,  "Has a stationary point"], ["no1", false, "No stationary point"]]; /* x^2 - 4x */
opt2: [["sp2", false, "Has a stationary point"], ["no2", true,  "No stationary point"]]; /* 3x + 2  */
opt3: [["sp3", true,  "Has a stationary point"], ["no3", false, "No stationary point"]]; /* x^2 + 1 */
opt4: [["sp4", false, "Has a stationary point"], ["no4", true,  "No stationary point"]]; /* 5 - 2x  */
    ]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
  <specificfeedback format="html"><text>[[feedback:prt1]]</text></specificfeedback>
  <questionnote format="html">
    <text>Classify: x^2-4x (SP), 3x+2 (none), x^2+1 (SP), 5-2x (none).</text>
  </questionnote>
  <questiondescription format="html"><text><![CDATA[This question is original to this kit and does not come from a source text. It is the reference example of classifying objects with a diagnostic for a named misconception.]]></text></questiondescription>
