      <falsefeedback format="html"><text></text></falsefeedback>
    </node>
    <node>
      <name>1</name>
      <description>Diagnostic: linear function called "has a stationary point"?</description>
      <answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest>
      <sans>lin_err</sans>
      <tans>1</tans>
      <testoptions></testoptions>
      <quiet>1</quiet>
      <truescoremode>=</truescoremode>
      <truescore>0.0000000</truescore>
      <truepenalty></truepenalty>
      <truenextnode>-1</truenextnode>
      <trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote>
      <truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Not quite. A linear function such as \(3x + 2\) or \(5 - 2x\) has a <strong>constant gradient</strong>: its derivative is a non-zero constant (\(3\) and \(-2\) respectively). Since the derivative is never zero, a linear function has <strong>no stationary point</strong>. A function has a stationary point only where its derivative equals zero.</p>]]></text></truefeedback>
      <falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode>
      <falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore>
      <falsepenalty></falsepenalty>
      <falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode>
      <falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote>
      <falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Not quite. A function has a stationary point exactly where its derivative is zero. Differentiate each function and check whether the derivative can equal zero: the quadratics turn around at a minimum, while a linear function has a constant gradient that is never zero.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback>
    </node>
  </prt>

  <qtest>
